README file for:

Uncle Bernie's rendering of the official Microsoft 6502 BASIC Source code

This rendered source code has been translated from DEC MACRO-10 syntax to a
syntax that is compatible with most modern 6502 macro assemblers, or can easily
be adapted to any macro assembler of your choice.

Parts which were missing from the original source code and associated switches
were added so the rendered source code can generate a matching binary for KIM-1,OSI Superboard II, PET 2001 and Apple II plus.

See the 'MSB6502.asm' source file for more information on how this was done and
how the target systems can be chosen.

CHOICE OF THE DASM ASSEMBLER - BUT IT NEEDS UPGRADES

I started this work using the 'ATASM' assembler which served me well for many
decades and was used for all my 6502 coding work including all the work I did
for the Apple-1. Alas, ATASM crashes with a 'Segmentation Fault' whenever live
code (6502 instructions) is assembled into the zero page. As I don't have the
time to fix such bugs in third party software, I looked for a close enough
relative of 'ATASM' and found 'DASM', which is quite popular in the Atari VCS
coder community (yes, there are still people out there who write new games for
the VCS, one of the earliest 6502 based TV game consoles). It also looks as if
'DASM' grew out of 'ATASM' or at least used 'ATASM' as an inspiration. Another
plus for DASM is that it can be compiled on many host systems, not only on
Linux, which is very helpful. Alas, 'DASM' lacks some features of 'ATASM'.

So, assembly of 'MSB6502.asm' in the presented from requires the DASM assembler
and some minor upgrades to DASM itself which are trivial and clean, but were
rejected by the DASM team, who spent more time on arguing with me than what they
would have needed to put my ready made upgrades I gave to them into DASM.

NICE TO HAVE UPGRADES OF DASM (AND WHY) BUT NOT BEING MANDATORY

In particular, they disliked my added features to allow line numbers in the
source file, and the '|' character to identify block nesting levels which IMHO
are impossible to follow for human readers unless such a 'crutch' is provided.

I had to write an automatic tool to parse the original source code and to
translate it into the initial rendered form with the more modern .IF / .ELSE /
.ENDIF constructs. The MACRO-10 bracketing for code blocks used '<' and '>'
which is unreadable for humans as soon as the nesting level gets too deep.

However, this added feature is not mandatory, as I've provided an alternate
source file 'MSB6502.alt' without line numbers and the '|' helpers for the
nesting level. It is just more difficult to read and the missing line numbers
which refer to the lines in the original source file are lost, which makes any
reference to the original source file more tedious and error prone.
(You can see that my added features indeed do have their merits for this task)

MANDATORY UPGRADE TO DASM - ADD TWO LINES IN DASM SOURCE CODE

One added feature for DASM is mandatory, and this is the introduction of the
DCX directive. Which like DC takes a string, and dumps it into the object code,
but it sets the MSB of the last character, a feature which the original DASM
lacks, but is required for the assembly of Microsoft 6502 BASIC.

Adding DCX to DASM only involves adding two lines of code, one line each in the
source files globals.c and ops.c and for me it is unfathomable why the DASM team
would no do this. Maybe they are afraid of the code in ops.c which IMHO is quite
weird and suspicious. It looks like a bunch of hacks which were piled upon an
earlier very crude and primitive version of the v_dc() function and no one dares
to touch it anymore. I did dare to do this:

UPGRADED DASM EXECUTABLE PROVIDED

The "ub_dasm" executable in the tarball has all the upgrades, but it might not
run on all versions of Linux, depending on 32 bit vs 64 bit machine and which
libraries are present. If you run into such a problem, or if you want to avoid
