HOW TO GET AND UPGRADE DASM

The DASM source code can be obtained from:

https://github.com/dasm-assembler/dasm

and in my own experience it compiles fine and without any problems on Linux.
After you got a successful compile, add the upgrades found in subdirectory
DASM_UPGRADE of this tarball. Despite I have provided '.ed' files, do not use
'patch' but do the edits to main.c, global.c and ops.c manually ... this will
save you pitfalls with future versions of DASM where line numbers etc. may be
different. (It would have been much easier for all of us if the DASM team had
decided to add my upgrades, but you may try to nudge them into doing that, by
going to the above github page, click on 'Issues' and open a new one, and tell
them (politely) that you want 'Uncle Bernie's upgrades' included in the main
source tree of DASM. My initial request was fruitless, see here:

https://github.com/dasm-assembler/dasm/issues/157

I still wonder why ... "Not invented here syndrome" ? Being afraid of the weird code in their 'ops.c' ? - - - it can't be lack of time because they spent more
time writing up arguments to reject my request than it would have taken them to
adopt my ready made and absolutely perfect and bug free source code upgrades.

MAKING TOOLS AND TESTING YOUR INSTALLATION

Once you have put the upgraded DASM executable into the directory with the rest
of this tarball, enter the commands:

make tools
make tests

and watch out if there are any complaints. You want to see a *** MATCH *** after
each target. You can also give the command:

ll *.diff

after the run, and there should be no such files. If a diff file is found, then
there was a mismatch between the reference file (name.ref) and the hexdump file
(name.hex) generated for the target with 'name'. The use of hexdumps helps you
to find where the problem is. For binary files, 'diff' only tells you if the
binary files are different or not. This is not very helpful.

CLEANING UP

The command:

make clean

will remove all the files generated by the test run.

GENERATING ROM IMAGES FOR EPROM BURNING OR FOR EMULATORS

This capability is not provided in this tarball, because it's not needed for the
educational purpose of this work. My intent was to provide you with a modernized
and better human readable version of Microsoft's original source code, so you
can study it to gain understanding how Microsoft 6502 BASIC works.

IMHO it is pointless to use this work to make yet another set of ROM images,
because all of them can be found on the web and all emulators for KIM-1, OSI,
PET and Apple II come with such images, which contain the same code as could be
generated by using this work.

But, if you want to port Microsoft 6502 BASIC to another machine, you need to
write a small tool to generate a proper binary from the output of DASM. This
is because the source code produces some 'active' code in the zero page, and
DASM fills the gap between this code and the actual start of the BASIC inter-
preter with filler bytes. Which need to be removed (along with the zero page
stuff) to obtain a proper, lean ROM image. To write this little tool, you can
start with the 'hexdump.c' source provided in the tarball.

There is one exception: for the OSI crowd, I've provided a binary image of ROM3
which includes my own "Garbage Collection Bug" fix. This is ROM3.BIN in the OSI
subdirectory. Use at your own risk only. I do not claim it works under all cond-
itions. But back in 1980 when I coded that fix, no better fix was known.

TRANSLATION TO OTHER MACRO ASSEMBLERS

If you want to translate this work to another 6502 macro assembler, feel free to
do so. The only condition I have is that you keep all my comments in the source
code as is, and that you don't claim that you did all this work all by yourself.
You may add a comment such as:

 Translation for the XXXXX macro assembler by YOUR_NAME

... BEFORE the comment box with 'Uncle Bernie's edition ...' and AFTER the line
number 0007 of the original Microsoft source code. This is the following line:

0007         ORG     0             ; START OFF AT LOCATION ZERO.

Also, try to keep all the stuff your assembler chokes on intact, just comment it
out, in the same way I did it with the MACRO-10 commands which are not needed
nor understood by DASM.

Don't forget to publish your translation.

